ally think there has to be  recognition of the work that people did  to close the digital divide to do  something that never been done before  back in april as the pandemic was  horrendous  to put these devices in the hands of  kids and i i don't quite  understand why the same question keeps  being asked when in fact the evidence is  that immense work was done to provide  devices to address any problem with  devices to provide  service if they need a different service  that work has literally  been done now family by family for kids  in shelters  that sometimes it's true that we don't  have as many devices as we  want to but the  the bottom line is it's just been an  endless effort  to provide free technology  to any family who needs it and that's  never stopped and it's been  consistent and i think it deserves uh  respect  and the people who've done the work have  really put their heart and soul into  making sure  kids got what they needed go ahead uh  chancellor  uh thank you mr mayor i will only add  marcia that uh  you know since march we did we've  delivered uh ordered and delivered over  350 000 devices  uh as students have devices sometimes  it's their own devices it's devices  they've gotten from the school those  devices break then it becomes a need for  another device  we've ordered over 100 000 devices again  we have about 40 000 of those devices  that are arriving on a daily basis  then we're inserting lte cards into  those devices so that students are able  to connect to the internet  so this is a constantly moving number  it's not a set number  that remains static and then nothing  happens so you feel it or you don't feel  this is a constantly evolving  uh situation and the other thing that i  would mention is that there is a real  supply side  issue here we are not the only school  system in america  actually in the world that is ordering  computers and devices  so the manufacturers are having a hard  time filling these orders  and while we've been pr